# Git
.git
.gitignore
.githooks

# CI/CD
.github

# Build artifacts
bin/
# ...except the one path the Dockerfile consumes. `bin/` is the local build
# output directory (`go build -o bin/cidx`, the Makefile), and it stays ignored
# so a developer's leftovers -- cross-compiled binaries, `go test -c` output,
# whatever else accumulates there -- never reach the build context. But the
# image is *built from* bin/cidx, so excluding the whole directory left
# `COPY bin/cidx` matching nothing. kaniko does not fail on that: it stats the
# context directory before applying .dockerignore, sees the file, then filters
# it out while copying, and exits 0 having copied nothing. Every published
# image was a bare docker CLI with a dead ENTRYPOINT (#281). Dropping `bin/`
# outright would fix the COPY and re-open the hygiene hole; re-including the
# single consumed path fixes it and keeps the hole shut.
!bin/cidx
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary
*.test

# Output of the go coverage tool
*.out

# Documentation
docs/
*.md
!README.md

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

